Performance improvement of fully ambient air fabricated perovskite solar cells in an anti-solvent process using \(\mathbf{TiO}_{2}\) hollow spheres
\(^{1}\)Department of Physics, Faculty of Science, Shahid Chamran University of Ahvaz, Ahvaz, Iran E-mail: mozhgan.talebi@gmail.com, m.seyedtalebi@sr.u.ac.ir Tel: 00989123094967; Fax: 00986133331040
Keywords: Anti-solvent; Carbon sphere; Mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\); Perovskite Solar cell; \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) hollow sphere.
## 1. Introduction
In general, perovskite solar cells can be assembled as either planar heterojunction architectures [1-8] or mesoscopic structures [9–13]. It has been reported that mesoscopic structure with an inorganic metal oxide semiconductor as the electron transporting layer (ETL) has attracted much attention. Due to the transportation of electrons through both of perovskite layer and mesoporous ETL, design of the ETL plays a crucial role in the efficiency of mesoscopic perovskite solar cells not only as a scaffold of perovskite absorber but also as the pathway for transporting electrons and blocking the holes [14- 18]. In the present work, advanced interface engineering has been applied by porosity modification of commonly used \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer as a more efficient ETL in the normal perovskite solar cells, which helps more \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) penetration into the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) lattice and excellent pore filling through the highly interconnected mesoporous structure. The resultant ETL which is not exactly mesoporous helps to obtain a high efficient and stable PSCs based on the combination of planar and mesoporous structures. In comparison with a standard solar cell, more easy mass migration results in the growth of perovskite deep inside the modified \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer and obtaining a perovskite layer with larger crystal size and more electron injection [19, 20].
The combination effect of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) and MAI in the crystallization and morphology of mesoscopic \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) based PSCs discussed in previous reports [21-26]. In the conventional solution processes, the porous structure of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) hindered the diffusion of \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) into the pore channel of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) filled by \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\), resulting in relatively small amounts of unreacted \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) remaining in the finally obtained perovskite solar cell [27]. Therefore, crystallization process maybe results in some large crystals on the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) surface but poor coverage due to the localized nucleation of perovskite layer which tends to obstruct the continual diffusion of MAI to react with \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) [28]. The fast combination of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) and MAI at the outside capping layer, leading to a small contact area between perovskite and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer. In addition, it seems most of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) is converted to the perovskite, but some amounts of unreacted \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) may be retained in the interface of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)/perovskite layer. The residual \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) has a detrimental effect
on carrier separation and blocks the transformation of electrons from \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) to \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) inducing a decrease in PCE of PSCs [28, 29]. In comparison with solution methods, in the anti-solvent treatment technique can largely improve the coverage of a compact perovskite layer with uniform grain structure up to micrometers on the surface of ETL [30]. The anti-solvent deposition in the air pressure is preferred here due to all of the fabrication process performed using a simple lab heater. Thus, the results are more applicable for industrial production of solar panels by a higher degree of reproducibility and easily generating of an extremely uniform and flat film with no any pinhole and uncovered area on the surface.
This work is organized as follows: It first summarizes the synthesis and characterization of carbon sphere templates and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) - HSs that we used. It then outlines the effect of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) - HSs usage on the properties of perovskite layers deposited on different ETLs. Finally, the characterization results of modified solar cell are compared with the standard perovskite solar cell.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2. 1. Sample preparation
In this section, the characterization of carbon spheres, and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) - HSs were firstly explained. The preparation of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -HSs and effects of their usage on the morphology and grain size of the perovskite layer were then investigated. In the end, the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) - HSs paste was used to build perovskite solar cell according to the procedure described in the experimental section.
## 2. 2. 1. Characterization of \(\mathbf{TiO}_{2}\) hollow sphere
Preparation of carbon sphere from sucrose as precursor using hydrothermal method and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) - HSs using carbon sphere templates were described in detail elsewhere [31, 32]. Figure 1 demonstrates typical field emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) images of
synthesized carbon spheres and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) - HSs. The obtained HSs have a regular shape with an average diameter of about \(160\pm30\;\mathrm{nm}\).
The experimental analysis of elemental mapping (MAP) pattern, and energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) of the samples shown in Figure 2.
