## 2. 1. 2. Preparation of \(\mathbf{TiO}_{2}\) hollow sphere paste
The \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) hollow sphere paste was synthesized from \(\alpha\)-terpineol as a solvent, and ethyl cellulose as a binder. The preparation details can be found in the Supporting Informations. The pores with uniform size distribution in the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) hollow sphere paste as effective light scattering centers extend the light traveling distance, thereby improve the optical absorption of the ETL.
## 2. 2. PSC devices fabrication and measurement techniques
As shown in the schematic illustration of Figures 3(a), 3(b), the layered structure of present perovskite solar cells consist of a compact layer of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) deposited on top of F-doped \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) and a pure or modified \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer. The mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer filled with a perovskite layer and Spiro as a hole-transport layer. More details about the fabrication process can be found in the Supporting Informations.
## 2. 2. 1. Fabrication of PSCs
In this study, we fabricated a compact and pin-hole free perovskite film with full coverage on the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer via one step method using nonpolar diethyl ether as anti-solvent solution while spinning in the atmospheric pressure [30]. Images of perovskite deposition process using this method is shown in Figure 3(c-e).
The FESEM images of deposited \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) films on both mp- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) - HS films (Figure 4) show rather homogeneous surfaces with no indications of pinhole defects. Fine tuning porosity of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) films using \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) – HSs results in very well penetration of MAI into the pores of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and creating a uniform, continuous, and homogeneous compact \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) film with a complete surface coverage by larger grain sizes. Perovskite film with larger grain size causes to mitigate the possibility of trap states or carrier blocking at grain boundaries. Perovskite devices fabricated on such highly porous layers with better pore-filling of perovskite show higher PCE and diminished hysteresis.
As shown in Figure 4, all of the deposited \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) layers have compact structure, but the size of the \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) grains are obviously different. For the \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) layers deposited on the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), the average size of \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) grains is approximately \(800\ \mathrm{nm}\)
(Figure 4(a)). Using \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) HSs, the average size of \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) grains increased to 1300 nm.
Relatively lower defect density observed in fabricated perovskite on the modified ETL as compared to that on the standard ETL could also be attributed to the slow crystallization rate inside the scaffold. During the perovskite formation process, the color of the coated precursor on the modified ETL undergoes slower color change as compared to the one fabricated on the standard mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) substrate, indicating the slow crystallization of perovskite film. A slow crystallization rate allows for a high-quality perovskite crystal with lower defect density. The XRD pattern of perovskite films is shown in figure 4(c). The appearance of main diffraction peaks at 2θ: 14.18, 28.45, 31.81, and \(43.08\,^{\circ}\) can be assigned as the (110), (220), (310) and (330) plans of tetragonal \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) crystals, respectively. The absence of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) peak at \(12.6\,^{\circ}\) shows the complete conversion of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\). The observed XRD peak positions of \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) crystals match well with the previously reported data [33-35]. The intensities of main tetragonal phase peaks are significantly enhanced for \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) layer deposited on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -HS film (Figure 4(c)). This implies the favourable crystallographic orientations for carrier transport perpendicular to the substrate increase upon the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) optimization which is important to obtain a uniform, compact, and smooth perovskite layer
with lesser voids to avoid unnecessary leakage of current [36]. Interestingly, the (110) peak shift of \(\sim0.04\,^{\circ}\) toward a lower angle is observed for fabricated perovskite on modified ETL, can be attributed to the volume extension without formation of additional recombination centres [37]. This volume expansion might be ascribed to the tensile stress developed by the attachment of the growing perovskite crystal to the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -HSs while constraining the shrinkage during crystallization. The slightly enlarged estimated band gap of modified perovskite (about \(0.01\ \mathrm{eV}\)) shown in insert of figure 4 (d) can be attributed to the crystal volume expansion of fabricated perovskite on the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -HSs. Owing the higher absorbance of deposited perovskite on the \(\mathrm{HS-TiO}_{2}\) ETL, it is expected for the PSK on the \(\mathrm{HS-TiO}_{2}\) film to generate higher photocurrent in the modified perovskite solar cell device.
## 2. 2. 2. PL and TRPL of \(\mathbf{CH}_{3}\mathbf{NH}_{3}\mathbf{PbI}_{3}\) layer on different ETLs
The steady-state photoluminescence (PL) measurements performed to confirm the better charges separation, charges extraction, and recombination resistance between ETL and perovskite absorber by application of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) – HS as an efficient ETL.
Although the position of PL peaks for all of the \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) films deposited on the different substrates is approximately centred at a constant wavelength of \(\sim770\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 5 (a)), when the \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) layer was in contact with and without \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layers showing different PL intensities with order of PSK> PSK- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\,>\,\mathrm{PSK}\) - \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -HS, indicating the electron extracting ability in the reverse order. The strongest PL quenching has been observed in \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) layer deposited on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) –HS film due to the existence of more recombination resistance and electron extracting ability than other samples. The great PL quenching caused by the improved interconnection on the interface of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) –HS / \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) due to more effective electron-injection from perovskite to ETL.
The corresponding band gap \((\mathrm{E_{g}}{=}1.61\ \mathrm{eV})\) determined from the PL spectra of modified perovskite (Figure 5(a)) is in good agreement with the previous researcher reports [38-40]. This observation also is supported by the determined band gap from UV-vis absorption spectra using Tauc plot (Figure 4 (d)).
Time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) decay profiles were studied to investigate the effect of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) –HSs on the lifetime of charge carriers. In Figure 5 (b), the PL decay of the perovskite films on glass without and with various ETLs was compared. The observance of higher PL quenching in the case of deposited \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) on the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -HS surface than the standard \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer, indicates more electron-extracting ability of the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -HS surface. The fitted lifetimes with bi-exponential decay equations and two components under different substrates are listed in Table 1. The fast decay components \(\left(\tau_{1}\right)\) are attributed to the charge carrier recombination and trap-assisted recombination related to the interface (or surface) defects. The slow components \(\left(\tau_{2}\right)\) are ascribed to the radiative recombination related to the bulk defects. Coated perovskite on glass substrate exhibited the longest decay components for both fast and slow recombination \(\tau_{1}{=}\,39.3\) ns and \(\tau_{2}\,{=}127.3~\mathrm{ns}\) ), indicating its highest non-radiative  recombination  centers. The  fitted  bi-exponential  decay  equation  for
\(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) film on standard mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer resulted in an effective lifetime with a fast component of \(\tau_{1}=3.5\) ns and a slow component of \(\tau_{2}=16.3\) ns. Assuming that all PL quenches are due to the effect of electron extraction, the time of interfacial electron extraction \(\left(\tau_{\mathrm{e}}\right)\) at the interface between perovskite layer and ETL can be calculated as:
\[
\frac{1}{\uptau_{\mathrm{e}}}=\frac{1}{\uptau_{\mathrm{PSK/ETL}}}-\frac{1}{\uptau_{\mathrm{PSK}}}
\]
in which \(\tau_{\mathrm{psk}}\) and \(\tau_{\mathrm{PSK/ETL}}\) are the PL lifetimes for perovskite layer deposited on glass substrate, and those with perovskite deposited on standard and modified ETLs, respectively. The average PL lifetime of perovskite layer \(\left(\tau_{\mathrm{PSK}}\right)\) was determined using the statistical definition \(\tau_{\mathrm{PSK}}=\frac{\sum A_{i}\tau_{i}}{\sum A_{i}\tau_{i}}\) to be 97.8 ns, in which \(\tau_{\mathrm{i}}\) and \(\mathrm{A}_{\mathrm{i}}\) are lifetimes and corresponding amplitudes of each component, respectively.
As one can see in Table 1, the \(\tau_{\mathrm{e}}\) in the interface of perovskite and ETL decreases from 13.2 to 7.4 ns when perovskite layer was in contact with the standard and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -HSs based ETL, respectively. This implies that the trend of electron extraction \(\left(1/\tau_{\mathrm{e}}\right)\) in the \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}/\mathrm{ETL}\) interface of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -HSs based solar cell is higher than that of the standard one, indicating that \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) –HSs based ETL as a more efficient ETL is responsible for the high-performance \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) based solar cells. The revealed change in the slow component of modified cell can be indicative of the suppression of trap-assisted recombination and improvement of the quality of the bulk perovskite. These results are in agreement with the FESEM observations
of figure 4(b) in which standard perovskite layer has a defect density and grain boundaries higher than that of the modified perovskite layer. The enhanced charge carrier extraction with reduced trap density can explain the improved PCE along with less hysteresis for the HS-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) based perovskite solar cells.
## 2. 3. Photovoltaic study results
The experimental results of current density-voltage characterization of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and HS- \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) based perovskite solar cells are listed in Table 2. The statistical analysis of data demonstrated good reproducibility of devices. Figure 6 compares the distributions of \(\mathrm{J_{sc}}\), \(\mathrm{V_{oc}}\), fill factor, and PCE of best devices of each type under the same experimental conditions with various ETLs.
The improved FF and \(\mathrm{J_{SC}}\) of samples made of modified \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) films by \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) –HSs are attributed mainly to the larger \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) grains and uniform covering the entire substrate,
which improve light-harvesting properties. The larger fill factor shows that the modification on the surface reduces the loss of photo-excited carriers being extracted from the device due to the suppression of surface electron recombination. The increase in short circuit current density is also evidence for better light absorption and light scattering of modified ETL by \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-HSs. Higher charge concentration in the interface of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and perovskite absorber for a certain number of injected electrons leads to retarding the recombination in PSC and getting high \(\mathrm{V_{oc}}\) and PCE.
Our results indicate that photovoltaic performance of the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) – HS based device shows the greater \(\mathrm{V_{OC}}\), \(\mathrm{J_{SC}}\), FF, and PCE values than those of standard device which reasonably explains the superior shorter electron-extraction time and higher photocurrent response of the perovskite deposited onto the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-HS layer than that of on the standard ETL.
The best performing devices with modified \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer show the PCE up to \(19.62\%\) with very small hysteresis in J-V characteristics. Enlarging the interfacial contact between perovskite and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) by effective pore-filling suppresses the charge accumulation caused by ion migration and diminishes the possibility of hysteresis. The J-V characteristics of the
\(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) based solar cells prepared with varying standard ETL by \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) –HSs in forward and reverse scans are shown in Figure 7. Although the hysteresis effect in regular planar structures is greater than that of PSCs with mesoporous ETL [14]. In this structure, the hysteresis effects have been significantly relieved compared with the standard device with mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer. Reducing the hysteresis effect (Figure 7 (b)), is related to trapping and detrapping mechanism of electrons at the interface of perovskite and modified ETL.
The majority of located electron and hole traps near the surface and grain boundaries of the perovskite layer could be a reason for the reduction of ion migration at grain boundaries and J-V hysteresis. The observance of hysteresis in the perovskite solar cells is in accordance with the modification of the interfacial surfaces and time-dependent photocurrent density (Jsc) behavior shown in Figure 8.
As observed in the hysteresis curves, at certain voltages for both standard and modified devises, the J–V curves at forward and backward stepwise scan separate from each other. This effect is clearly visible at \(\sim0.7{-}0.9\) open circuit voltage. The discrepancy in the J–V characteristic of the hysteresis curves varies trends, consequently, results in the scanning direction-dependent of photocurrent density behavior of devices. For the time-dependent
photocurrent density measurements, the devices were kept under one sun illumination (AM 1.5, \(100\;\mathrm{mW}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\)) at \(0.8\;\mathrm{V_{oc}}\).
In the reverse scan (RS) curves, the detected current increases sharply as a large photocurrent spike while sudden increase in the photo induced separation of the electron hole pairs occurs. As charge carriers migrated from the interior of perovskite layer to the interfaces (electrons to the ETL and holes to the HTL), the photocurrent spike followed by a slow exponentially decay with time to the equilibrium state. When the recombination rate and charge generation reach to equilibrium, the steady state current is achieved. The subsequent decrease in photocurrent indicates that more recombination is occurring within the perovskite layer. The smaller difference in observed photo current density of modified devise (\(\sim\!1.34\;\mathrm{eV}\)) in spite of \(\mathrm{J_{sc}}\) difference monitored in the time dependent \(\mathrm{J_{sc}}\) of standard devise (\(\sim\!1.63\;\mathrm{eV}\)) is due to the less hysteresis behavior of \(\mathrm{HS-TiO}_{2}\) based solar cell.
Under forward stepwise scan (FD) with possessing the applied bias, detected current shows a sharp decrease in the time-dependent photocurrent density at first, followed by a continuous recovery and reaches to the saturation point. The \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-HSs as the electron transporting layer provided high photocurrent due to better connection between ETL/perovskite layer, and growing the smooth perovskite layer on its surface than the standard ETL substrate.
The impedance spectroscopy (IS) measurement of standard and modified solar cells with different ETLs is performed to monitor the interfacial changes such as charge transfer, recombination, and charge accumulation in the interfaces of perovskite layer. The Nyquist plots of the standard and modified solar cells, which are measured under one-sun illumination and open-circuit conditions in the frequency range from 1 MHz to 1 Hz shown in Figure 9. In the corresponding equivalent circuit of Figure 9, \(R_{\mathrm{s}}\), \(R_{\mathrm{con}}\) and \(R_{\mathrm{rec}}\) are related to the series resistance, the contact resistance at perovskite's interfaces, and recombination resistance, respectively.
The contact resistance \((R_{\mathrm{con}})\) derived from high-frequency arc in the Nyquist plots literally means the charge transfer resistance at the ETM/perovskite/HTM interfaces. It can be related
to the electron transporting ability of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer resulting from the geometrical factors such as roughness and the structure of the mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer. Thus, the contact resistance value significantly changed when porosity modification of ETL is involved because of better contact and easier electron transfer at the interface of perovskite and modified ETL. The \(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{con}}\) value is decreased for the modified solar cell compared to the standard solar cell (from 290 \(\Omega\) to \(189.5\ \Omega\)) which is indicative of better contact between \(\mathrm{HS}-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and perovskite in comparison to that between \(\mathrm{mp}-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and perovskite. Moreover, the \(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}\) is significantly higher for modified solar cell (78.73 \(\Omega\) compared to 62 \(\Omega\)) which pointed to the more blocked charge recombination and more electrons extraction at the interface of perovskite/ETL of modified solar cell, which leads to the improvement of photovoltaic characteristics.
## 3. Conclusions
Our research focused on the importance of morphology control of ambient air processed perovskite layer through substrate surface modification to obtain highly efficient PSC device, which has an impact on developing PSC's commercialization in the future. We have fabricated a \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-HS based ETL with similar thickness of \(\mathrm{mp}-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) in order to demonstrate the morphology and porosity of ETL play a crucial role to attain a satisfactory device performance and decreased hysteresis for PSC. The \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) based ETL designed with \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-HSs leading to formation of a dense, uniform and homogeneous perovskite layer, which ensures smooth surface with low defect density and maximum contact areas at the interfaces with the PSK/ETL; it thus enhances charge separation at the interface of PSK/ETL. The characterization of deposited perovskite on the standard \(\mathrm{mp}-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer and modified layer with \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) – HSs was investigated with FESEM, EDX-mapping, and UV-vis analysis. Moreover, the enhancement in photocurrent of \(\mathrm{HS}-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) based PSC is relatively higher than response of solar cell with \(\mathrm{mp}-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer due to the enormous contact surface area between \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{HS}-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\). This is supported by the smooth perovskite layer seen in FESEM results. The electron-transfer properties were investigated via steady state PL and TRPL (via TCSPC) spectra. We found a correlation between the rate of electron transfer and the
morphology of mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer. When the perovskite layer was coated on the mp-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer, ETL exhibited a PL lifetime longer than that of the HS-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) based substrate, representing much slower charge transport in a standard ETL. The best device performance attained PCE 19.62\,\% for \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-HS based PSK, at which larger perovskite grain size and rapid electron transfer occurred. The \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-HS based devices also showed a minor effect of hysteresis with excellent reproducibility during a fully air processed fabrication. As our ETL structure is something between mesoscopic and planar, we believe the idea is valuable for readers to test it in other devices and check the efficiency enhancement of their standard cells in air atmosphere and pressure for commercial application.
